WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.438
Welcome to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:09.768
This is Chapter Two, the Danish democracy, Episode One: the constitution, Grundloven.

3
00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:12.280
How does a country run itself?

4
00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:18.268
That is the question this whole chapter answers, and we start with the document that sets the rules of the game.

5
00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:23.493
The Danish democracy has developed over more than one hundred and seventy-five years.

6
00:00:23.493 --> 00:00:27.956
It has been shaped by events and political ideas in Denmark and abroad.

7
00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:37.578
The Danish system of government, the folkestyre, has a lot in common with other European democracies, but it has also found its own special form.

8
00:00:38.128 --> 00:00:39.766
Here is the foundation.

9
00:00:39.716 --> 00:00:47.291
Danish democracy is built on a written constitution: Danmarks Riges Grundlov, usually just called Grundloven.

10
00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:53.691
It deals especially with national democracy, as it takes place in the Folketing, the Danish parliament.

11
00:00:53.691 --> 00:00:58.791
It contains rules about the Folketing, about the government, and about the courts.

12
00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:06.684
The constitution sets certain limits on how the state may use its power, and it secures the citizens a number of fundamental rights.

13
00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:12.421
But Denmark also has a strong tradition of local democracy and self-government.

14
00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:16.184
Local self-government has deep roots in Danish history.

15
00:01:16.184 --> 00:01:22.459
The constitution gives the municipalities, the kommuner, a role in looking after local matters.

16
00:01:22.459 --> 00:01:29.547
Local self-government in the municipalities gives citizens the chance to help decide many things in the local community.

17
00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:33.822
And local democracy shapes many very close everyday matters too.

18
00:01:33.822 --> 00:01:46.662
In a school board, in a housing association, and in various committees at a workplace, you can, as a parent, a resident or an employee, get influence on things that concern your everyday life.

19
00:01:47.212 --> 00:01:53.475
The Danish democracy has many different players, each with their own roles, views and interests.

20
00:01:53.425 --> 00:01:57.537
Organisations and associations are also part of political life.

21
00:01:57.537 --> 00:02:16.125
That can be labour market organisations such as the Danish Trade Union Confederation, FH, and the Danish Employers' Confederation, DA, or business or environmental organisations, which all consist of a long list of trade unions, employers' associations and interest organisations.

22
00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:22.824
The Folketing, the Supreme Court and several ministries are all located on Slotsholmen in Copenhagen.

23
00:02:23.374 --> 00:02:26.137
Now let's open the constitution itself.

24
00:02:26.087 --> 00:02:33.137
The framework of Danish democracy is laid down in Danmarks Riges Grundlov, which is Denmark's constitution.

25
00:02:33.137 --> 00:02:42.212
It is a fairly short text, with a total of eighty-nine paragraphs, which contain the basic rules and principles of the Danish state's form of government.

26
00:02:42.212 --> 00:02:47.874
The constitution also secures the population a number of fundamental rights and freedoms.

27
00:02:48.424 --> 00:02:54.286
Denmark got its first democratic constitution on the fifth of June, 1849.

28
00:02:54.236 --> 00:02:57.799
That is why this date is Constitution Day, Grundlovsdag.

29
00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:08.411
For almost two hundred years before that, since sixteen sixty to sixty-one, the country had been ruled by an absolute king on the basis of the King's Law of 1665.

30
00:03:08.411 --> 00:03:14.268
When the constitution was adopted, the king's power was shared with elected representatives.

31
00:03:14.818 --> 00:03:20.218
The constitution of 1849 is a foundation that has since been gradually built on.

32
00:03:20.168 --> 00:03:27.293
Only with the Change of System in 1901 did the king have to accept that the Folketing decides who should have government power.

33
00:03:27.293 --> 00:03:29.886
This system is called parliamentarism.

34
00:03:30.436 --> 00:03:32.073
Who could vote at the beginning?

35
00:03:32.023 --> 00:03:34.323
Only men over thirty years old.

36
00:03:34.323 --> 00:03:39.573
They must not have received poor relief and must not have been declared legally incapable.

37
00:03:39.573 --> 00:03:43.548
They also had to have a fixed place of residence and be of good repute.

38
00:03:43.548 --> 00:03:51.798
Women and servants, that is, people without their own household, who served and lived with another family, did not get the vote.

39
00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:55.748
This only happened with the constitution of 1915.

40
00:03:55.748 --> 00:04:06.264
Since then, the voting age for the Folketing has gradually been lowered from thirty years down to the present voting age of eighteen years, which was adopted in 1978.

41
00:04:06.814 --> 00:04:08.901
Now, a set of key dates.

42
00:04:08.851 --> 00:04:16.439
The constitution has been changed in 1866, 1915, 1920 and 1953.

43
00:04:16.439 --> 00:04:20.326
The current constitution is therefore from 1953.

44
00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:25.051
Much has changed in Denmark over the last one hundred and seventy-five years.

45
00:04:25.051 --> 00:04:33.906
But many of the principles, and large parts of the text of the newest constitution, go all the way back to the constitution of 1849.

46
00:04:34.456 --> 00:04:40.593
It is often emphasised that the constitution is a firm but roomy framework around Danish democracy.

47
00:04:40.543 --> 00:04:48.300
It is worded so generally that political life has been able to change without it being necessary to change the provisions of the constitution.

48
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:55.350
The constitution is meant to help secure political stability and protect citizens' fundamental rights.

49
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.362
That is why it has been made very difficult to change.

50
00:04:58.362 --> 00:05:04.150
The constitution can only be changed if there is support from both the Folketing and the population.

51
00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:14.910
That ensures, among other things, that a random political majority cannot take a minority's fundamental rights away from it, or introduce a completely different form of government.

52
00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:21.510
The constitution of 1953 still bears the mark of the language of 1849.

53
00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.672
It uses words and expressions that are not common today.

54
00:05:24.672 --> 00:05:30.960
If you read the constitution, you can easily get the impression that the king still plays a big political role.

55
00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:38.310
But the rules of the constitution are interpreted in practice in such a way that the king's power has been taken over by the government.

56
00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:46.385
The language of the constitution is one of the reasons why it has at times been debated whether the constitution should be changed to fit the present better.

57
00:05:46.385 --> 00:05:57.160
It has also been discussed whether it should be better at protecting basic freedoms and human rights, and whether the rules for Denmark's participation in international cooperation should be changed.

58
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:04.010
Many, however, think there is no reason to change a well-functioning constitution that allows adaptation to the times.

59
00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:11.585
In fact, it is very difficult, and perhaps almost impossible, to change the constitution because of the strict requirements.

60
00:06:11.585 --> 00:06:21.312
The fifth of June is called Constitution Day and is celebrated every year as a national day of celebration, with political meetings all over the country.

61
00:06:21.862 --> 00:06:24.249
So what does the constitution say?

62
00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:27.424
The constitution is the highest law in Denmark.

63
00:06:27.424 --> 00:06:33.587
The Folketing and the government must therefore respect it, and must not do anything contrary to it.

64
00:06:33.587 --> 00:06:37.530
It sets the most important rules of the game for national democracy.

65
00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:47.467
The constitution contains rules about: the state's highest bodies, the Folketing, the government and the courts, and the relationship between them.

66
00:06:47.417 --> 00:06:49.817
The election of members of the Folketing.

67
00:06:49.817 --> 00:06:51.492
The adoption of laws.

68
00:06:51.492 --> 00:06:54.842
The Danish state's relationship to the outside world.

69
00:06:54.842 --> 00:06:58.455
The Danish national church, the folkekirke.

70
00:06:58.455 --> 00:07:00.155
Local self-government.

71
00:07:00.155 --> 00:07:02.676
And the citizens' fundamental rights.

72
00:07:03.226 --> 00:07:05.901
Denmark has a representative democracy.

73
00:07:05.851 --> 00:07:12.313
That means the population elects representatives to the Folketing, who then make decisions on behalf of the people.

74
00:07:12.313 --> 00:07:15.826
The elected stand accountable to the voters on election day.

75
00:07:15.826 --> 00:07:21.463
It is the voters who decide whether the individual party or politician should be elected again.

76
00:07:21.463 --> 00:07:25.826
Democracy in Denmark is thus predominantly an indirect democracy.

77
00:07:25.826 --> 00:07:28.501
However, referendums are also held.

78
00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:33.294
Here the population can directly take a stand on a specific political question.

79
00:07:33.844 --> 00:07:37.494
In Danish democracy, parliamentarism applies.

80
00:07:37.444 --> 00:07:44.156
That means a government cannot be formed, or stay in power, if it has a majority in the Folketing against it.

81
00:07:44.156 --> 00:07:51.144
Since the voters decide how the Folketing is composed, the voters also decide who should have government power.

82
00:07:51.144 --> 00:07:57.672
In 1953, this parliamentary principle was written into the text of the constitution itself.

83
00:07:58.222 --> 00:08:05.509
The power of the state is divided in three: a legislative power, an executive power and a judicial power.

84
00:08:05.459 --> 00:08:13.497
This separation of powers was introduced with the constitution of 1849, which abolished the king's absolute power.

85
00:08:13.497 --> 00:08:20.272
The separation of powers means that the government and the Folketing together make laws, the legislative power.

86
00:08:20.272 --> 00:08:28.272
It is the government that, through the ministries, administers the laws in practice and puts them into effect, the executive power.

87
00:08:28.272 --> 00:08:31.672
The third power is the courts, the judicial power.

88
00:08:31.672 --> 00:08:36.309
They settle conflicts between citizens, and between citizens and the state.

89
00:08:36.309 --> 00:08:39.954
It is also the courts that pass judgment in criminal cases.

90
00:08:40.504 --> 00:08:46.417
The basic idea is that the three powers should control and limit each other's exercise of power.

91
00:08:46.366 --> 00:08:54.841
The separation of powers is, in other words, meant to ensure that power in society does not become centralised or misused.

92
00:08:54.841 --> 00:08:58.942
The division protects citizens against arbitrary interference.

93
00:08:58.942 --> 00:09:04.428
This separation of powers exists in different versions in most democratic countries.

94
00:09:04.978 --> 00:09:06.803
Now, the head of state.

95
00:09:06.753 --> 00:09:14.728
Denmark's head of state is a monarch, that is, a king or, as in the period 1972 to 2024, a queen.

96
00:09:14.728 --> 00:09:19.291
The monarch's position is inherited and does not give any political power.

97
00:09:19.291 --> 00:09:27.990
The monarch and the other members of the royal family have especially a number of ceremonial tasks, and tasks as representatives of Denmark.

98
00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:34.253
The royal house represents, for example, official Denmark in relation to foreign heads of state.

99
00:09:34.253 --> 00:09:39.166
The political power is exercised solely by the government and the Folketing.

100
00:09:39.166 --> 00:09:45.303
It is the same in other European monarchies like Sweden, Norway, Belgium and Great Britain.

101
00:09:45.303 --> 00:09:52.853
So Denmark does not have an elected, and politically powerful, head of state like the presidents of France and the USA.

102
00:09:52.853 --> 00:09:59.266
Queen Margrethe the Second, born 1940, reigned from 1972 to 2024.

103
00:09:59.266 --> 00:10:08.574
A photo in the chapter shows her receiving Ukraine's president Volodymyr Zelensky and his wife Olena on a state visit to Denmark in 2023.

104
00:10:09.124 --> 00:10:16.037
The constitution also contains a rule that Danish citizenship, indfødsret, can only be granted by law.

105
00:10:15.987 --> 00:10:23.424
That means it is the government and the majority of the Folketing who decide which foreigners can be granted Danish citizenship.

106
00:10:23.974 --> 00:10:29.687
The constitution also secures and protects several fundamental freedoms and human rights.

107
00:10:29.637 --> 00:10:37.874
This includes the right to freely express your opinions under responsibility before the courts, freedom of expression, ytringsfrihed.

108
00:10:37.874 --> 00:10:42.462
The right to meet with others, freedom of assembly, forsamlingsfrihed.

109
00:10:42.462 --> 00:10:44.562
And the right to form associations.

110
00:10:44.562 --> 00:10:52.924
The constitution also protects personal freedom, freedom of religion, the right of property, and the privacy of private life.

111
00:10:52.924 --> 00:10:59.850
These rights protect the individual citizen, and the minority, against the majority misusing its power.

112
00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:02.650
Here is your recap for Episode One.

113
00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.113
Denmark's constitution, Grundloven, has eighty-nine paragraphs.

114
00:11:07.113 --> 00:11:15.438
The first democratic constitution was adopted on the fifth of June, 1849, so the fifth of June is Constitution Day.

115
00:11:15.438 --> 00:11:24.913
It has been changed in 1866, 1915, 1920 and 1953, and the current one is from 1953.

116
00:11:24.913 --> 00:11:29.400
It can only be changed with support from both the Folketing and the population.

117
00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:33.175
Women got the vote with the constitution of 1915.

118
00:11:33.175 --> 00:11:37.063
The voting age of eighteen was adopted in 1978.

119
00:11:37.063 --> 00:11:41.850
The parliamentary principle was written into the constitution in 1953.

120
00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:49.550
The power of the state is divided into the legislative, executive and judicial powers, introduced in 1849.

121
00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:55.575
Denmark has a representative democracy, with a monarch as head of state without political power.

122
00:11:55.575 --> 00:11:59.028
And Danish citizenship can only be granted by law.

123
00:11:59.578 --> 00:12:02.178
Now it is time for your exam practice.

124
00:12:02.128 --> 00:12:07.041
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

125
00:12:07.041 --> 00:12:14.526
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

126
00:12:15.076 --> 00:12:16.326
Question 1.

127
00:12:16.276 --> 00:12:18.676
When was the constitution last changed?

128
00:12:18.676 --> 00:12:21.314
Option A: 1849.

129
00:12:21.314 --> 00:12:23.614
Option B: 1920.

130
00:12:23.614 --> 00:12:26.352
Option C: 1953.
[DA] Hvornår blev grundloven sidst ændret?  A: 1849  |  B: 1920  |  C: 1953

131
00:12:32.902 --> 00:12:35.677
The answer is C: 1953.

132
00:12:35.627 --> 00:12:38.226
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: 1953

133
00:12:38.776 --> 00:12:40.026
Question 2.

134
00:12:39.976 --> 00:12:42.701
When was Denmark's Constitution introduced?

135
00:12:42.701 --> 00:12:45.339
Option A: 1849.

136
00:12:45.339 --> 00:12:47.826
Option B: 1814.

137
00:12:47.826 --> 00:12:50.508
Option C: 1789.
[DA] Hvornår blev Danmarks Riges Grundlov indført?  A: 1849  |  B: 1814  |  C: 1789

138
00:12:57.058 --> 00:12:59.896
The answer is A: 1849.

139
00:12:59.846 --> 00:13:04.014
It appeared in the November 2020 and May 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: 1849

140
00:13:04.564 --> 00:13:05.902
Question 3.

141
00:13:05.852 --> 00:13:08.502
Which day is celebrated on the fifth of June?

142
00:13:08.502 --> 00:13:11.027
Option A: The Queen's birthday.

143
00:13:11.027 --> 00:13:13.852
Option B: Denmark's liberation.

144
00:13:13.852 --> 00:13:17.280
Option C: Constitution Day, Grundlovsdag.
[DA] Hvilken mærkedag fejres den 5. juni?  A: Dronningens fødselsdag  |  B: Danmarks befrielse  |  C: Grundlovsdag

145
00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:27.430
The answer is C: Constitution Day, Grundlovsdag.

146
00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.066
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Grundlovsdag

147
00:13:30.616 --> 00:13:31.916
Question 4.

148
00:13:31.866 --> 00:13:34.404
Which day is Constitution Day in Denmark?

149
00:13:34.404 --> 00:13:37.041
Option A: The fifth of June.

150
00:13:37.041 --> 00:13:39.679
Option B: The fifteenth of June.

151
00:13:39.679 --> 00:13:42.540
Option C: The twenty-third of June.
[DA] Hvilken dag er det grundlovsdag i Danmark?  A: Den 5. juni  |  B: Den 15. juni  |  C: Den 23. juni

152
00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.828
The answer is A: The fifth of June.

153
00:13:51.778 --> 00:13:54.558
It appeared in the June 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Den 5. juni

154
00:13:55.108 --> 00:13:56.571
Question 5.

155
00:13:56.521 --> 00:14:00.833
Through which constitutional amendment did women get the vote for the Folketing?

156
00:14:00.833 --> 00:14:04.408
Option A: The amendment of 1866.

157
00:14:04.408 --> 00:14:07.796
Option B: The amendment of 1915.

158
00:14:07.796 --> 00:14:11.520
Option C: The amendment of 1953.
[DA] Ved hvilken grundlovsændring fik kvinder valgret til Folketinget?  A: Grundlovsændringen i 1866  |  B: Grundlovsændringen i 1915  |  C: Grundlovsændringen i 1953

159
00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:21.795
The answer is B: The amendment of 1915.

160
00:14:21.745 --> 00:14:27.522
It appeared in the November 2020, June 2021 and June 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Grundlovsændringen i 1915

161
00:14:28.072 --> 00:14:29.510
Question 6.

162
00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:33.635
What was adopted with the constitutional amendment of 1915?

163
00:14:33.635 --> 00:14:37.597
Option A: That women could inherit the Danish throne.

164
00:14:37.597 --> 00:14:40.997
Option B: That women got the vote for the Folketing.

165
00:14:40.997 --> 00:14:44.412
Option C: That women had to serve conscription.
[DA] Hvad blev vedtaget med grundlovsændringen i 1915?  A: At kvinder fik mulighed for at arve den danske trone  |  B: At kvinder fik stemmeret til Folketinget  |  C: At kvinder skulle aftjene værnepligt

166
00:14:50.962 --> 00:14:54.650
The answer is B: That women got the vote for the Folketing.

167
00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.270
It appeared in the May 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: At kvinder fik stemmeret til Folketinget

168
00:14:57.820 --> 00:14:59.258
Question 7.

169
00:14:59.208 --> 00:15:03.745
Did women get the vote for the Folketing with the constitution of 1849?

170
00:15:03.745 --> 00:15:05.370
Option A: Yes.

171
00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.032
Option B: No.
[DA] Fik kvinder valgret til Folketinget med grundloven i 1849?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

172
00:15:13.582 --> 00:15:15.582
The answer is B: No.

173
00:15:15.532 --> 00:15:20.370
It appeared in the November 2023 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Nej

174
00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:22.183
Question 8.

175
00:15:22.133 --> 00:15:26.883
Between whom are the legislative, the executive and the judicial power divided?

176
00:15:26.883 --> 00:15:31.008
Option A: The Folketing, the government and the courts.

177
00:15:31.008 --> 00:15:34.858
Option B: The Folketing, the regions and the courts.

178
00:15:34.858 --> 00:15:39.564
Option C: The government, the regions and the municipalities.
[DA] Hvem er den lovgivende, den udøvende og den dømmende magt fordelt mellem?  A: Folketinget, regeringen og domstolene  |  B: Folketinget, regionerne og domstolene  |  C: Regeringen, regionerne og kommunerne

179
00:15:46.114 --> 00:15:50.402
The answer is A: The Folketing, the government and the courts.

180
00:15:50.352 --> 00:15:55.302
It appeared in 4 different exams, most recently in November 2025.
[DA] ✔ A: Folketinget, regeringen og domstolene

181
00:15:55.852 --> 00:15:57.227
Question 9.

182
00:15:57.177 --> 00:15:59.027
Who holds the judicial power?

183
00:15:59.027 --> 00:16:01.315
Option A: The Folketing.

184
00:16:01.315 --> 00:16:03.415
Option B: The government.

185
00:16:03.415 --> 00:16:05.592
Option C: The courts.
[DA] Hvem har den dømmende magt?  A: Folketinget  |  B: Regeringen  |  C: Domstolene

186
00:16:12.142 --> 00:16:14.392
The answer is C: The courts.

187
00:16:14.342 --> 00:16:17.034
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Domstolene

188
00:16:17.584 --> 00:16:18.897
Question 10.

189
00:16:18.847 --> 00:16:21.409
Which right is secured in the constitution?

190
00:16:21.409 --> 00:16:24.084
Option A: The right to a holiday.

191
00:16:24.084 --> 00:16:27.184
Option B: The right to own hunting weapons.

192
00:16:27.184 --> 00:16:30.372
Option C: The right to express oneself.
[DA] Hvilken rettighed er sikret i grundloven?  A: Retten til at holde ferie  |  B: Retten til at eje jagtvåben  |  C: Retten til at ytre sig

193
00:16:36.922 --> 00:16:40.172
The answer is C: The right to express oneself.

194
00:16:40.122 --> 00:16:44.694
It appeared in the November 2022 and June 2026 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Retten til at ytre sig

195
00:16:45.244 --> 00:16:46.719
Question 11.

196
00:16:46.669 --> 00:16:49.232
Which right is secured in the constitution?

197
00:16:49.232 --> 00:16:52.244
Option A: The right to form an association.

198
00:16:52.244 --> 00:16:55.032
Option B: The right to carry a weapon.

199
00:16:55.032 --> 00:16:57.768
Option C: The right to a holiday.
[DA] Hvilken rettighed er sikret i grundloven?  A: Retten til at danne en forening  |  B: Retten til at bære et våben  |  C: Retten til at holde ferie

200
00:17:04.318 --> 00:17:07.568
The answer is A: The right to form an association.

201
00:17:07.518 --> 00:17:10.194
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Retten til at danne en forening

202
00:17:10.744 --> 00:17:12.144
